Z||000002||Old Lar||11-16-2001||06:42 AM||||Movies about war are fanaties, although "Full Metal Jacket" brought back some bad memories. <P>I don't know of any attempts of rescue after the US pulled out in 1975.  And thee are always a few Rambo/Chuck Norris wannabes, but they are rather low keyed people who didn't get the publicity, so no one knows if anything happened. I think Ross Perot paid a bunch of money to send in some rescuers back, but I don't think they were very succssful.||||reg||1
Z||000003||johnt671||11-16-2001||08:18 AM||||Air Force commandos raided a prison camp in North Vietnam while I was there.  They got into the camp but they moved the prisoners a day or two before.  I believe that there were several attempts shortly after the war was over by people to get into North Vietnam and investigate stories of pows still held and found evidence that there were but the US and NV governments both deny it.  Read an interesting book about that a few years ago but the name escapes me at the moment.  I was a Marine and it is true that we are trained to never leave anyone behind.  Sometimes we didn't have a lot let to send home but we did it.  That was one of the reasons I joined the Corps.  For anyone who is interested, Full Metal Jacket was  very accurate as far as the Basic Training part went.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Formula88||11-16-2001||11:02 AM||||Well, not really a story about Rambo going in to rescue POW's, but I think you'll like this:<P>A good friend of mine, in honor of Vietnam POWs has worn the wrist band of a U.S. soldier MIA in Vietnam.  "Smiley" (I don't remember his real name, this was his callsign) was listed as missing during the war.  My friend wore this armband for about 7 years, if I remember correctly.  In doing some research, she found out that he had indeed survived and made it home to the U.S.  She contacted him and told him about herself.  They made arrangements to meet, and he took of her armband himself.  And his name is listed on the Wall.<P>Never Forget.<BR>God Bless America!||||reg||6
Z||000005||Mach10||11-16-2001||01:08 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by johnt671:<BR><B>Air Force commandos raided a prison camp in North Vietnam while I was there.  They got into the camp but they moved the prisoners a day or two before.  I believe that there were several attempts shortly after the war was over by people to get into North Vietnam and investigate stories of pows still held and found evidence that there were but the US and NV governments both deny it.  Read an interesting book about that a few years ago but the name escapes me at the moment.  I was a Marine and it is true that we are trained to never leave anyone behind.  Sometimes we didn't have a lot let to send home but we did it.  That was one of the reasons I joined the Corps.  For anyone who is interested, Full Metal Jacket was  very accurate as far as the Basic Training part went.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>The Drill Sargent is my HERO  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>I'M GONNA SKULL-&^%& YOU!!<P>||||reg||10
Z||000006||Formula88||11-16-2001||01:18 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Mach10:<BR><B>The Drill Sargent is my HERO   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>I'M GONNA SKULL-&^%& YOU!!<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>R. Lee Ermy, the guy who played the Drill Sergent is an ex-Marine himself (no big surprise there).  He's not far from what you saw in FMJ.  Check out the movie "The Seige of Fire Base Gloria."  He stars in it, as himself.  It's his story about being caught at Fire Base Gloria during the Tet Offensive.  Incredible movie.  He, and I think one other person, were the only survivors of that seige.||||reg||1
Z||000007||ray b||11-16-2001||01:35 PM||||rambo is hollywood BS no POW ever got out of N-viet, choppers tryed but failed in raid on empty camp.<BR>anyone read mcgovern was right by cal tomas??<BR>was in local paper yesterday,as cal is a rightwing nut i never thought he would see the truth about viet war and war againts war we fought on the home front<BR>but his last lines say<BR>"the johnson tapes should teach conservatives a lesson. many anti-war activists love this country as much as those who supported the viet-nam war. just because someone isof a different party or persuasion does not necessarily mean they are wrong"<P>------------------<BR>Question wonder and be wierd||||reg||1
Z||000008||maryjane||11-16-2001||08:58 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Mach10:<BR><B>Quick! Before MJ gets here:<BR>I never left, had no answer to original question.<P>MARINES NEVER LEAVE ANYONE BEHIND!<BR>Careful!! You're walking on hallowed ground here. Remember the link I sent to the 'team littlegull' story? Actually, many military units try to follow this doctrine, including 101st Army Airborne & Navy Seals. We just practice it fanatically.<P>Seriously though, I'm not sure. I know that the US Marines have a reputation for the above, dead, or alive. Brave mo'fos.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>It's not so much bravery as it is reliance and respect for fellow Marines. We're a small outfit, compared to the other branches. We learned early on that we HAD to rely on each other. "They'd do it for me-I'd do it for them" We have to fill the shoes of those who walked before us, from Tripoli to Lebanon. The Chosin Reservoir-Korea. In the early days of that battle, , Gen Macaurthur's representative met with CG of 1st Mar Div, O.P. Smith, and offered to fly the men of the 1st Mar Div out if they would leave all their equipment and non walking wounded and dead. Reply was," We'll fight our way out, or the 1st Marine Division will never fight as a unit again." Rest is history. They brought out almost everyone, alive & dead, went on to fight in the second defense of Seoul. The Army unit on the East side of the Chosen(Task Force Faith) did not fare nearly as well-was all but destroyed. A victim of poor leadership, terrible planning, no battle experience, but mostly, no confidence. I worked with the Army's Americal Div in VietNam, I have a lot of respect for that bunch. <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000009||johnt671||11-16-2001||09:08 PM||||Marine legend Chesty Puller at the frozen Chosen when told the marines were surrounded and vastly outnumbered by the Chinese "Good.  Now we can shoot in any direction and kill the sob's.||||reg||1
